 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 66,
   "y": 11,
   "plaque": "akrotiri_dhekelia_raf",
   "title": "RAF Akrotiri",
   "text": "RAF Akrotiri is a Royal Air Force station on the Akrotiri peninsula, built in the 1950s and one of the RAF's most important bases overseas. Its long runway has served as a stepping stone for flights from Britain to the Middle East for generations, and as a base for operations in the region. Most of the people who work here are in uniform; most of the people who help them are Cypriot."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 20,
   "plaque": "akrotiri_dhekelia_sba",
   "title": "The Sovereign Base Areas",
   "text": "When Cyprus became independent in 1960, the United Kingdom kept two areas of the island as Sovereign Base Areas: Akrotiri in the south-west and Dhekelia in the south-east, about 254 square kilometres in all. The Treaty of Establishment promised that they would stay military bases, with no colonies and no commercial development. A great many Cypriots live and work in the villages inside them."
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 26,
   "plaque": "akrotiri_dhekelia_episkopi",
   "title": "Episkopi",
   "text": "Episkopi is a British garrison town inside the Akrotiri Sovereign Base Area, with a church, a school, a NAAFI shop and a lot of palm trees. The families who live here are British service families and the Cypriot staff who work alongside them. Its name means 'bishopric' in Greek, which tells you it had a church long before it had a garrison."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 38,
   "plaque": "akrotiri_dhekelia_salt_lake",
   "title": "The Akrotiri Salt Lake",
   "text": "The salt lake is a shallow lagoon behind the Akrotiri peninsula, much of which dries to a white salt crust in summer. In winter it fills with rain, and thousands of greater flamingos, ducks and waders arrive from the north. The salt was harvested here for centuries, and the pink colour is from a tiny shrimp and salt-loving algae."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 27,
   "plaque": "akrotiri_dhekelia_flamingos",
   "title": "The Flamingos",
   "text": "The greater flamingo is a tall, pink bird that feeds by sweeping its curved bill through the shallow water and filtering out shrimp. The shrimp's pigments are what turn the flamingos pink. The Akrotiri Salt Lake is one of the best places to see them in the Mediterranean, and the birds stand on one leg to save warmth, and because they can."
  },
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 50,
   "plaque": "akrotiri_dhekelia_turtles",
   "title": "The Turtle Beaches",
   "text": "The Akrotiri beaches are nesting sites for the green turtle and the loggerhead. The females come ashore at night in summer, dig a pit, and lay about a hundred eggs. The hatchlings emerge two months later and run for the sea, guided by the horizon's brightness. Lights on the beach can lead them the wrong way, so the beach has quiet hours."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 54,
   "plaque": "akrotiri_dhekelia_ladys_mile",
   "title": "Lady's Mile",
   "text": "Lady's Mile is a long sand beach on the south coast of the Akrotiri peninsula, backed by dunes and a lagoon, and a favourite with swimmers, windsurfers and walkers. In spring the dunes are green with plants that have learnt to survive on salt and wind."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 49,
   "plaque": "akrotiri_dhekelia_cats",
   "title": "St Nicholas of the Cats",
   "text": "The monastery of St Nicholas of the Cats, on the Akrotiri peninsula, takes its name from a legend. In the 4th century, Cyprus suffered a drought and a plague of snakes, and Saint Helena, the mother of the Emperor Constantine, is said to have sent a shipload of cats from Egypt to kill them. The monastery kept the cats, and it still has them."
  },
  {
   "x": 66,
   "y": 38,
   "plaque": "akrotiri_dhekelia_kourion",
   "title": "Kourion",
   "text": "Kourion was one of the ancient city-kingdoms of Cyprus, perched on a cliff above the sea. Its Greco-Roman theatre was first built in the 2nd century BC and rebuilt several times, and seated about 3,500 people. It is still used for summer performances, with the sea behind the stage."
  },
  {
   "x": 66,
   "y": 52,
   "plaque": "akrotiri_dhekelia_eustolios",
   "title": "The House of Eustolios",
   "text": "The House of Eustolios was a grand villa at Kourion, with a bath suite and floors of mosaic. The mosaics date from the early Christian period, and a Greek inscription reads, roughly, that there was no place there for envy. The house was built about the 5th century, after the city had begun to decline."
  }
